  <Patch Id="FJzZkKqVfW12i9kQk6L0hz">
    <Canvas Id="N0E8bKG5vqG1aLaGsfcogC" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--
    ************************ Application ************************
-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="Pt4eKK7Ag0dikcitT4Vmar">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="T1yChjTcVpcQLJ4rxMEL44">
        <Canvas Id="VxYLn5bECvkjhyGerEE3sc" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="VaZNqihOnpwz6dORWEdpu0" Bounds="-2515,-66,860,1180" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="07 - Your own points, lines and polygons.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Tokyo Haneda. Everything on this map was typed in as numbers - no file is read anywhere.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THREE KINDS, AND THEY ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE:&#xD;&#xA;  Point        where something IS          the three terminals&#xD;&#xA;  LineString   the shape of a way          runway 16L/34R&#xD;&#xA;  Polygon      how much ground             a box somebody drew&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Coordinate ------------------------------------&gt; Point&#xD;&#xA;  Coordinate x2 -&gt; Cons -------------------------&gt; LineString&#xD;&#xA;  Coordinate x4 -&gt; Cons -&gt; LinearRing -----------&gt; Polygon&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A line is an ordered list of coordinates. An area is a ring, and a ring is a list that comes back&#xD;&#xA;to where it started. LINEARRING CLOSES ITSELF - you gave it four corners and it made a ring of&#xD;&#xA;five, the last the same as the first. That is what makes a ring a ring, and NTS does it so you&#xD;&#xA;cannot forget to.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;CHECK YOUR OWN WORK, AGAINST THE MAP. The runway is real published aviation data, and&#xD;&#xA;OpenStreetMap draws Haneda's runways too - so your line should lie exactly along the grey strip&#xD;&#xA;underneath it. Change one digit and watch it slide off. Coordinates typed by hand usually have&#xD;&#xA;nothing to be wrong against; here they do, and that is worth more than any assurance from me.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;PULL A WIRE OUT. Disconnect StyleByGeometry's Polygon pin. The box vanishes - and Layer Status&#xD;&#xA;names the pin you unplugged. The style is chosen by each feature's TYPE, not by the order you&#xD;&#xA;built things in, and not by anything you say. That is the whole idea of the node.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;TWO OF THESE ARE REAL AND ONE IS NOT, on purpose:&#xD;&#xA;  the terminals   Wikidata, CC0&#xD;&#xA;  the runway      OurAirports, public domain&#xD;&#xA;  the box         I drew it. It is not the boundary of anything.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A real airport boundary has hundreds of vertices and arrives in a file - that is chapter 09.&#xD;&#xA;Building geometry by hand is for the other case: a rough area, a sketch, a thing you are inventing&#xD;&#xA;because no one has measured it yet. KNOWING WHICH CASE YOU ARE IN is most of what this chapter is&#xD;&#xA;for.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NOW SWITCH ON &quot;SHOW THE REAL AIRPORT&quot;, which is the other half of this chapter.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Underneath your five features drops the actual thing: 754 features out of OpenStreetMap - 712&#xD;&#xA;taxiways, 14 aprons, 9 runways, 9 terminals, 6 hangars, 4 helipads. Your runway lands on theirs.&#xD;&#xA;Your three points sit on their three terminal buildings.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THAT GAP IS THE LESSON, and it is why chapter 09 exists. Nobody types 754 features. You type five&#xD;&#xA;when five is what you have: a sketch, an area nobody has surveyed, a thing you are inventing. You&#xD;&#xA;load a file when the world has already been measured by somebody else.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Notice how the file gets read. FileReader is fired by ONOPEN, not every frame - without that, 307&#xD;&#xA;KB comes off the disk sixty times a second. And File Path says Assets\haneda.geojson, RELATIVE to&#xD;&#xA;this document, which is the only reason it still works on your machine. It must never be left&#xD;&#xA;EMPTY: an empty Path is not &quot;no path&quot;, it means this document's own folder, and that mistake once&#xD;&#xA;wrote 444 map tiles next to two git repositories while every check reported success.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;ADD MORE - the layer takes as many features as you hand it, and StyleByGeometry sorts them by type.&#xD;&#xA;Haneda's other three runways, same source:&#xD;&#xA;  16R/34L   139.768734 35.560452  -&gt;  139.785672 35.536591&#xD;&#xA;  04/22     139.761274 35.549015  -&gt;  139.777114 35.567459&#xD;&#xA;  05/23     139.803469 35.524001  -&gt;  139.822125 35.540598&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;OSM's own copy of 04/22 begins at 139.761264 35.549002. Ours, typed from a different source, says&#xD;&#xA;139.761274 35.549015 - about a metre apart. Two independent measurements of the same concrete.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;DEGREES ARE NOT METRES. Ask NTS for that polygon's Area and you get a number in square degrees,&#xD;&#xA;which is not a unit anybody wants. Nothing in this family reprojects yet; that is a known gap, not&#xD;&#xA;an oversight you have found.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHAT TO WATCH:&#xD;&#xA;  Layers Built   should reach 1 and stay. Moving the map must never change it.&#xD;&#xA;  Layer Status   how many features arrived, and which style pin is missing for them.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT - three directions, no order:&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 08          the map starts answering back: your cursor becomes a place&#xD;&#xA;  Prompt A mountain    the same craft on a mountain - nine peaks, typed by hand&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 02          these three kinds of geometry, before the earth arrived">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          
          
